24. Proximate versus Fundamental Causes of Growth
Paper: Economics of Growth and Development I (ECNCC302) | Unit: I | Topic: Proximate versus fundamental causes of growth
Introduction
Economists distinguish between the immediate sources of growth and the deeper reasons why some countries accumulate and innovate more than others. This distinction organises modern growth and development theory.
Proximate causes
Proximate causes are the direct inputs to production, captured in the growth accounting framework: Y = A F(K, L, H).
• Physical capital accumulation: investment and savings.
• Human capital: education, health, skills.
• Labour force growth.
• Technology and total factor productivity (TFP).
Growth accounting (Solow, Denison, Young) breaks output growth into contributions of these. Cross-country studies (Klenow and Rodriguez-Clare, Hall and Jones) show that TFP differences explain a large share, often more than half, of income gaps.
Fundamental causes
Fundamental causes explain why proximate factors differ.
1. Geography: climate, disease burden, access to ports, natural resources (Sachs, Diamond). Tropical locations face agricultural and health challenges.
2. Institutions: property rights, rule of law, constraints on elites, contract enforcement (North; Acemoglu, Johnson and Robinson). Colonial settler mortality shaped institutions: extractive versus inclusive. The "reversal of fortune" shows that once-rich regions became poor.
3. Culture and social capital: trust, work ethic, religion (Weber, Landes, Putnam).
4. Luck and history: multiple equilibria, path dependence, wars and shocks.
5. Integration and trade: openness (Sachs-Warner; Frankel-Romer), though Rodrik argues institutions dominate (Rodrik, Subramanian and Trebbi, "institutions rule").
6. Demography and human development.
Link between the two
Fundamental causes work through the proximate: good institutions encourage investment, innovation and schooling, raising K, H and A. Policies are an intermediate layer and can be changed more easily.
Policy implications
If proximate causes dominate, invest in capital and schooling; if fundamental causes dominate, reform institutions, governance and trade regimes, which are slower and harder to change.
Conclusion
Proximate causes tell us how growth happens; fundamental causes tell us why. Effective development strategy requires attention to both.